Implement a standard-library-only repository authorization policy in `solution.rs`.

Expose this exact API:

```rust
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct TrustPolicy {
    pub organizations: BTreeSet<String>,
    pub repositories: BTreeSet<String>,
}

#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum TrustChoice { Once, Remember, Reject }

pub fn normalize_repository(input: &str) -> Option<String>;
pub fn is_authorized(policy: &TrustPolicy, repository: &str) -> bool;
pub fn add_authorization(policy: &mut TrustPolicy, repository: &str) -> bool;
pub fn authorize_or_decide(policy: &mut TrustPolicy, repository: &str, interactive: bool, choice: TrustChoice) -> bool;
```

A valid repository has exactly two nonempty slash-separated components. Trim ASCII whitespace around the complete input and each component, lowercase both components using ASCII lowercase conversion, and return `organization/repository`. Inputs with extra slashes are invalid.

Authorization succeeds when the normalized full repository is present in `repositories` or its organization is present in `organizations`. Stored entries are already normalized. Invalid input is never authorized.

`add_authorization` inserts a valid normalized repository and returns whether the set changed. Invalid input leaves the policy unchanged and returns false. Repeated aliases that normalize to the same value must not create duplicates.

`authorize_or_decide` first accepts already-authorized repositories. Otherwise, invalid repositories and non-interactive calls return false without mutation. In interactive mode, `Once` returns true without mutation, `Remember` inserts the repository and returns true, and `Reject` returns false. Preserve all unrelated entries and expose no `main` function.

